WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:11.690
Function as a service. Container as a service. Database, identity, integration, backend, network, security as a service.

2
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:19.357
Here is the calm fact under the rain of acronyms: the official set is three.

3
00:00:19.357 --> 00:00:25.491
A new ‘as a service’ name does not create a fourth model.

4
00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:31.805
In September 2011, NIST defined software, platform, and infrastructure as a service.

5
00:00:31.805 --> 00:00:41.328
Its later evaluation guide sorts any offering by two facts: what is being provided, and who the primary customer is.

6
00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:50.850
An end user of a finished application — that is SaaS. A developer or deployer of an application — PaaS.

7
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:59.421
An operator of systems, who needs machines and networks — IaaS. Read every new name by that test.

8
00:01:00.021 --> 00:01:02.752
PaaS and SaaS had their own episodes.

9
00:01:02.752 --> 00:01:10.165
IaaS is the one left to describe, and from the microservice point of view it is the raw pool.

10
00:01:10.165 --> 00:01:19.139
The team can install a cluster, a broker, and a database engine, and run anything it can package; the provider runs the hosts.

11
00:01:19.139 --> 00:01:24.211
A virtual machine with a monolith on it is a completed IaaS deployment.

12
00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:29.674
A virtual machine with Kubernetes on it is a team-built platform sitting on IaaS.

13
00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:40.599
Managed Kubernetes moves part of that work back to the provider and lands between IaaS and PaaS — which is why the industry gave it a further name.

14
00:01:41.199 --> 00:01:50.091
Function as a service is PaaS narrowed to a function — Richardson’s serverless deployment; the function may be smaller than a bounded context.

15
00:01:50.091 --> 00:01:53.570
Containers as a service sits between IaaS and PaaS.

16
00:01:53.570 --> 00:02:00.916
Database as a service is the usual way to give a service its own database without operating the engine.

17
00:02:00.916 --> 00:02:08.648
Identity as a service is the access-token pattern, bought — and the tenant still has to be on the token.

18
00:02:08.648 --> 00:02:15.607
Integration platform as a service can become the smart bus the 2014 article kept out of the middle.

19
00:02:15.607 --> 00:02:24.500
Backend as a service is a shared backend behind client apps — easy to start, easy to become one database for every feature.

20
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.593
Storage and network as a service are IaaS.

21
00:02:27.593 --> 00:02:34.552
Security as a service is usually SaaS, and it does not replace the tenant check inside the aggregate.

22
00:02:34.552 --> 00:02:44.217
AI as a service is Pressman’s AI category consumed as a backing service — calling it is not the same as changing the service boundaries.

23
00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:55.873
Two names collide on DaaS: desktop as a service, and data as a service — in this series, the commercial form of a Data Mesh data product.

24
00:02:55.873 --> 00:03:00.377
The abbreviation alone does not say which one a document means.

25
00:03:00.377 --> 00:03:13.071
And a different list is easy to mix in: NIST’s four deployment models — public, private, community, hybrid — answer where the cloud runs, not what kind of product it is.

26
00:03:13.071 --> 00:03:16.347
Deployment model and service model are two columns.

27
00:03:16.947 --> 00:03:22.052
An AI suggestion that says ‘use a service’ has to name which model.

28
00:03:22.052 --> 00:03:30.298
Generating application code for an end user is SaaS work only if tenancy, metering, and the control plane are in scope.

29
00:03:30.298 --> 00:03:37.760
A container and a database binding are PaaS or CaaS work. A network and a machine are IaaS work.

30
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:46.006
Calling a hosted model is AI as a service — a backing service, not a new shape for the order aggregate.

31
00:03:46.606 --> 00:03:54.430
Three things to carry away. One. Three NIST models, sorted by who the customer is. Two.

32
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:59.809
Every newer ‘-aaS’ name lands under one of the three. Three.

33
00:03:59.809 --> 00:04:07.633
A hosted model is AI as a service — a backing service, not a new aggregate.

34
00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:17.696
Next: ‘the system shall be scalable’ is not yet a requirement. Functional, non-functional, and why services get split.
